RAWMCTL(1)

# NAME

rawmctl - inspect and control rawm desktop state

# SYNOPSIS

*rawmctl window list*

*rawmctl window activate* _window_

*rawmctl window select*

*rawmctl keyboard get*

*rawmctl keyboard set* _group_

*rawmctl -v*

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmctl* is the non-privileged command-line control surface for the rawm X11
session. It uses existing X11 authorities instead of reconstructing window
manager state from process lists or invoking external window-control tools.

Window inspection uses the EWMH *\_NET_CLIENT_LIST* root property. Activation
is requested with an EWMH *\_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW* client message. Keyboard group
inspection and control use the X Keyboard Extension directly.

*rawmctl* does not start, supervise, or configure *rawm*(1). Commands requiring
X fail when no display can be opened.

# WINDOW COMMANDS

*window list*
	List the windows currently admitted to *\_NET_CLIENT_LIST*. Each line has
	three tab-separated fields:

	```
	WINDOW<TAB>CLASS<TAB>TITLE
	```

	_WINDOW_ is printed as a hexadecimal XID. _CLASS_ is the WM_CLASS resource
	class. _TITLE_ prefers *\_NET_WM_NAME* and falls back to the ICCCM WM_NAME.
	ASCII control characters in class/title presentation are replaced by spaces
	so one managed window always occupies exactly one output line.

*window activate* _window_
	Request activation of the hexadecimal or decimal XID _window_. The XID must
	be present in the current *\_NET_CLIENT_LIST* snapshot. rawm follows the
	client to its admitted tag set when necessary, using its normal
	*\_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW* handling.

*window select*
	Read the current managed-window set, invoke *rawmenu*(1) in explicit
	*select* mode, and activate the selected XID. Selector rows begin with the
	XID followed by class and title presentation. The selected window is
	therefore identified by XID, not by title; duplicate window titles are
	unambiguous.

	*rawmenu* is executed directly through *execvp*(3) as:

	```
	rawmenu -M select -p Window:
	```

	No shell, *wmctrl*, *xdotool*, or *xdo* is involved. *rawmenu* must be
	available through PATH for this command only. Cancellation or rejection by
	the selector returns failure without changing the active window.

# KEYBOARD COMMANDS

*keyboard get*
	Print the current XKB core-keyboard group number.

*keyboard set* _group_
	Lock the XKB core keyboard to _group_, where _group_ is in the XKB range
	0 through 3. The command synchronizes with the X server and verifies that
	the resulting group is the requested one before returning success.

# EXIT STATUS

*0* on success; *>0* on invalid arguments, X11/XKB failure, an unmanaged window
identifier, selector cancellation/failure, or an unavailable required runtime
component.

# AUTHORITY BOUNDARY

The EWMH and XKB state used by *rawmctl* are desktop control mechanisms inside
the existing X11 trust domain; they are not authentication or privilege
boundaries. *rawmctl* deliberately does not introduce a private rawm command
protocol for operations that already have an established X11 authority.

Future rawm-specific control operations should acquire an explicit protocol
before being added rather than encoding ad-hoc commands in presentation
properties.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *rawmenu*(1), *X*(7)

